    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d389">

                <pb facs="0531" n="519" sameAs="frus:frus1943v03/pg519"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">701.6111/1179: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the Soviet
                        Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Standley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-05-01T22:00:00-04:00">May 1, 1943—10
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>275. Your 374 [<hi rendition="#italic">371</hi>], April 30, 11 a.m.<anchor n="60"
                        xml:id="d389-f1"/> Litvinoff is officially returning to Moscow for
                    consultation with his Government. He is leaving Madame Litvinoff here in the
                    United States. In private conversations with members of the Department he has
                    expressed disillusionment with the fact that he is at an important post with
                    little work to do since his Government has neither sent him any instructions nor
                    kept him informed of its policies.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d389-f1" n="60" xml:id="d389-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that Litvinov’s return had been announced in Moscow and
                    inquired whether the Department knew why he was returning.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
